  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 255 PM CST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nAn area of stratus is sitting over all of Minnesota and oozing \ninto northwest Wisconsin this afternoon, keeping temperatures \nmostly in the 40s, and may only get into the low 50 today. \nMeanwhile, where there has been sunshine all day over most of \nnorthwest Wisconsin, temperatures have already risen into the \nlow 50s, and should top out in the mid 50s. South to \nsouthwesterly winds this afternoon are quite gusty, rising into \nthe 20 to 25 mph range for many locations, with some of our more\nwind prone locations above that. A low pressure system moves \nfrom North Dakota northeast across Ontario through tomorrow. We \nare currently in the warm sector of this system and with a \ngeneral lack of moisture, we expect only these clouds for the \nrest of today and this evening. However, later tonight through \nThursday as the surface low passes north of the forecast area, \nwe should get some wrap-around lower clouds and chances for \nshowers and/or sprinkles. Shower and sprinkle chances have been \nincluded for about the northern half of the area starting early \nThursday morning, continuing through the day and then ending by \nearly evening. We are not looking at much for accumulation, \nperhaps a few hundredths at best.\n\nOur next weather system will move across the Northland Friday \nthrough the weekend. It is a much larger and deeper trough which\nmoves across the western CONUS and generates a surface low over\neastern Wyoming, which then ejects northeast across the \nDakotas, taking a track not too far off from the current system.\nStrong warm air advection continuing through Friday will keep \ntemperatures above normal with showers moving in from the west \nduring the late morning and afternoon. More widespread rainfall \nis expected Friday night, but mainly across the northwest half \nof the forecast area. A trailing cold front sweeps west to east \nacross the Northland late Friday night, Saturday and Saturday \nevening, keeping precipitation chances going, but with the \nhigher probabilities shifting to northwest Wisconsin. For now, \nPrice county has a 65% of at least a quarter inch of \nprecipitation for the 24 hour period ending 6 AM Sunday. We have\nbeen messaging the potential for snowfall accumulations for \nSaturday night through Sunday night. Much colder air diving into\nthe area behind the exiting storm system will support the \ntransition from rain to snow before the precipitation comes to \nan end. There are two areas most likely to receive accumulating \nsnow. The first is Northern Minnesota along and north of the \nIron Range, which mostly falls Saturday night into Sunday. \nSnowfall amounts of around an inch are possible along the \nUS/Canada border, with lighter amounts farther south. Lake \nEffect processes begin to develop for along the South Shore, \nwith snow most likely there from Sunday afternoon and night \nbefore ending early Monday morning. Accumulations there of an \ninch or so are possible there as well. \n\nA significantly cooler and more seasonable air mass will settle\ninto the area for the first half of the next work week. High \ntemperatures Monday through Wednesday will range from the upper \n20s to mid 30s, with overnight lows from 10 to 25 degrees. These\nwill be close to normal temperatures for the latter half of \nNovember. Dry and quiet weather is expected for the early part \nof the week under the influence of Canadian high pressure.",
